  "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: corruption entered U.S. policy in two ways. A client government's corruption became a reason Washington gave for withholding, conditioning or redesigning aid and a standing subject of pressure (Nationalist China 1944–48, South Vietnam 1960 and 1967, South Korea 1961); and from 1975 American companies' payments abroad, above all Lockheed's, put friendly governments at risk and pushed Washington toward an international agreement and the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act. Between them, bribery appears as an instrument used by rivals, proposed in Washington and charged against it (Jordan 1956, Iraq 1959, Iran 1965). The words appear in about one document in a hundred across the series' 553 volumes; 19 documents carry the argument. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came from the Department of State Central Decimal File (RG 59), the Marshall Mission Files (RG 59, Lot 54 D 270; ten NARA series under creator 10481258), the Conference Files (RG 59, Lot 62 D 181; probable series 602875, entry A1 3051B, 1949–1972, unrestricted), the Kissinger staff-meeting transcripts (RG 59, Entry 5177) and records (Entry 5403), and the Johnson, Kennedy, Ford and Carter libraries' national security files; the footnotes cite, unprinted, the Far Eastern Affairs files (RG 59, Lot 62 D 26; series 1965953, entry A1 3113, 1960–1963, partly restricted), the Saigon embassy's Can Lao file (RG 84, Lot 65 F 115) and the Jidda embassy's Lockheed file (RG 84, Lot 79 F 80). What this request cannot get from FRUS: the post-1980 record (the 1977 law is named in no document of the thirteen 1981–88 volumes); the Senate investigations' own records; the names of the payees, still withheld; and the nineteenth-century record, which is in the corpus but not followed here and has almost no shelf in the bundled finding aids.",
